  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of the Hall effect in a flat conducting slab placed in a magnetic field. A current, labeled \\(I\\), flows along the length of the sample, while a magnetic field \\(B\\) points perpendicular to the slab, along the vertical direction. Inside the material, several negative charge carriers are shown moving through the conductor and being deflected sideways along curved dashed paths. This illustrates the Lorentz force acting on moving charges in the presence of the magnetic field.\n\nBecause the carriers are pushed toward one side of the slab, charge builds up across the sample thickness or width, creating a transverse voltage marked \\(V_H\\). The circuit outline and voltmeter indicate that this Hall voltage is measured across the conductor, perpendicular to the current direction. The coordinate axes in the corner help define the geometry of current, magnetic field, and deflection directions.\n\nOverall, the image conveys the basic mechanism by which a magnetic field causes lateral separation of moving charges in a conductor, producing a measurable Hall voltage. The Lorentz-force equation shown at right summarizes the underlying physics.",
